ave no melds, and I'm discarding, maybe that's not feasible.\n\nLooking back, the best approach is to form four chows and a pair. The four chows would be in W and T suits. So if I can form W1-2-3, W2-3-4, W3-4-5, and T7-8-9, that's four chows. The remaining tiles would be W7, T1, T2, B1, B8. If I discard one of these, say W7, then after drawing, I might get a tile that can form a pair. For example, if I draw another T1 or T2, I can have a pair. But the unshown tiles for T1 is 4, T2 is 2. So there are still some left. So maybe discarding W7 is a good move because it allows me to form the four chows and then have a chance to pair T1 or T2.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard T2, then I lose the possibility of forming a chow with T1 and T2. So that's worse.\n\nLooking at the valid actions, the options include Play W7, which is part of the W5 and W7. If I discard W7, I can focus on the four chows in W and T. The T7-8-9 is a solid chow. The W1-2-3, W2-3-4, W3-4-5 would require overlapping tiles. Since I have W1, W2, W3, W4, W5, that's possible. So discarding W7 seems beneficial because it's not part of any chow, and keeping it might block forming a pair.\n\nAnother option is to discard T9. But that would remove the T7-8-9 chow, which is a key part of the hand. So that's worse.\n\nDiscarding W3 might be bad because it's part of multiple chows. Similarly for W4 or W5.\n\nSo the best choice is to discard W7. This allows the formation of four chows and leaves the T1, T2, B1, B8 to form a pair if possible. The unshown T1 and T2 have some left, so there's a chance to draw one and form a pair. This would give a Mixed Straight, which is worth 16 points, which is a solid score. Also, since I have no melds, it's a concealed hand, which gives 4 points. So total points would be 16 + 4 = 20, which is over the 8-point minimum. That's a good hand.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Discarding W7 to form four chows and potentially a pair in the T suit.\nReason: Discarding W7 allows forming W1-2-3, W2-3-4, W3-4-5 (three chows in W suit) and T7-8-9 (one chow in T suit), plus a possible pair from T1/T2 if drawn. This setup aims for a Mixed Straight (16 points) and Concealed Hand (4 points), totaling 20 points. W7 is not part of any chow and discarding it avoids blocking potential pair formation.\nAnswer: Play W7"}
